---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// DOI corrected: this page previously cited 10.1126/science.abn8197, which is Gao et al.,
// "The landscape of tolerated genetic variation in humans and primates" -- a different paper.
// Sullivan et al. is 10.1126/science.abn2937. Every number below is checked by
// `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug zoonomia`.
const title = "Leveraging Base-Pair Mammalian Constraint to Understand Genetic Variation and Human Disease";
const description = "Technical summary of Sullivan et al. (Zoonomia Consortium, Science 2023): single-base phyloP constraint from 240 mammals identifies 3.3% of the human genome as functional, with 7.8-fold SNP-heritability enrichment across 63 GWAS.";
const readingTime = "24 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Comparative Genomics & Evolutionary Constraint</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Science · 28 April 2023 (Zoonomia Consortium)</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Patrick F. Sullivan, Jennifer R. S. Meadows, Steven Gazal, BaDoi N. Phan, Xue Li … Zoonomia Consortium, Benedict Paten, Steven K. Reilly, Katherine S. Pollard, Andreas R. Pfenning, Elinor K. Karlsson, Kerstin Lindblad-Toh</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill; Uppsala University; Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard; UMass Chan Medical School; and the Zoonomia Consortium</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abn2937" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1126/science.abn2937</a> · Science 380(6643), eabn2937</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      GWAS has associated thousands of variants with human disease, but translating association into therapy
      requires knowing which of hundreds of candidates at a locus is causal. Sullivan et al. supply a prior
      that is <strong>agnostic to cell type, tissue and developmental stage</strong>: evolutionary constraint,
      measured base by base across <strong>240 mammals</strong> spanning ~100 million years. Single-base
      phyloP scores identify <strong>3.3% of the human genome as significantly constrained</strong> — including
      <strong>57.6% of coding bases</strong>, though <strong>80.7% of constrained bases are noncoding</strong>
      — and constrained regions carry <strong>7.8-fold enrichment of SNP-heritability</strong> across
      <strong>63 independent GWAS</strong>, more than nonsynonymous coding variants do.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. Constraint as a Function-Agnostic Annotation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#alignment">2. The 240-Mammal Alignment and Single-Base phyloP</a></li>
        <li><a href="#constrained-fraction">3. How Much of the Genome Is Constrained</a></li>
        <li><a href="#selection">4. Constraint Is Visible in Present-Day Human Variation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#heritability">5. The Headline Result: SNP-Heritability Enrichment</a></li>
        <li><a href="#scales">6. Informative at Every Scale, from 100 kb to One Base</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation: phyloP and Heritability Partitioning</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & What Constraint Cannot Tell You</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. Constraint as a Function-Agnostic Annotation</h2>
      <p>
        Every functional-genomics annotation answers a conditional question: this element is open in
        <em>this</em> cell type, bound by <em>this</em> factor, expressed at <em>this</em> stage. Miss the
        condition and you miss the element. ENCODE and GTEx are indispensable and are, structurally, bounded
        by the assays and tissues actually run.
      </p>
      <p>
        Evolutionary constraint sidesteps the conditional. If a base has accumulated unusually few
        substitutions across a hundred million years of mammalian evolution, something has been removing the
        mutations that hit it — <strong>regardless of which cell type, tissue or developmental stage the
        function operates in</strong>. The paper's framing is that constraint is a measure of functional
        importance that no assay design has to anticipate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="alignment" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. The 240-Mammal Alignment and Single-Base phyloP</h2>

      <h3>The resource</h3>
      <p>
        The analysis rests on an alignment of <strong>240 mammal genomes spanning roughly 100 million years of
        evolution</strong>. The scale matters in a specific way: constraint is detected by comparing observed
        substitutions against the neutral rate, so the statistical power to call a <em>single base</em>
        constrained scales with the total evolutionary branch length in the alignment. Fewer species, and
        constraint can only be called over windows.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> Why single-base resolution is the whole point
        </div>
        <p>
          A conserved <em>element</em> tells you a region matters. A constrained <em>base</em> tells you which
          nucleotide inside it matters — which is the resolution a fine-mapped GWAS credible set operates at,
          and the resolution at which a transcription factor motif is disrupted or preserved. The 240-genome
          alignment is what moves phyloP from the first regime into the second.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="constrained-fraction" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. How Much of the Genome Is Constrained</h2>
      <p>
        Using constraint calculated across placental mammals, <strong>3.3% of bases in the human genome are
        significantly constrained</strong>. Two decompositions of that number carry the argument:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Partition</th>
              <th>Value</th>
              <th>What it establishes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Constrained fraction of the genome</strong></td>
              <td><strong>3.3%</strong></td>
              <td>Function is rare, and far rarer than biochemical activity assays suggest</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coding bases that are constrained</strong></td>
              <td><strong>57.6%</strong></td>
              <td>Even within coding sequence, a large minority is tolerant — constraint is not a synonym for "coding"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Constrained bases that are noncoding</strong></td>
              <td><strong>80.7%</strong></td>
              <td>The overwhelming majority of functional bases sit outside genes, which is where GWAS hits sit too</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The two coding numbers point in opposite directions on purpose. Coding sequence is strongly enriched
        for constraint, and yet <em>most constrained bases are not coding</em> — because the noncoding genome
        is so much larger. Any variant-prioritisation scheme that treats "coding" as a proxy for "functional"
        loses four-fifths of the signal.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="selection" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Constraint Is Visible in Present-Day Human Variation</h2>

      <h3>Depletion of segregating variation</h3>
      <p>
        If constrained bases are under purifying selection, human polymorphism should avoid them — and it
        does. Common variants (allele frequency ≥ 5%) and low-frequency variants (0.5% ≤ AF &lt; 5%) are
        <strong>depleted for constrained bases: 1.85% versus 3.26% expected by chance</strong>, at
        <strong>P &lt; 2.2 × 10<sup>−308</sup></strong> — a p-value at the floor of double-precision
        representation, which is to say the depletion is not in doubt.
      </p>

      <h3>Clinical variants line up the same way</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Pathogenic ClinVar variants are more constrained than benign variants</strong>
        (<strong>P &lt; 2.2 × 10<sup>−16</sup></strong>). This is the external validation that matters: the
        constraint score was computed from mammalian alignments with no knowledge of human clinical
        annotation, and it separates the clinical classes anyway.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="heritability" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. The Headline Result: SNP-Heritability Enrichment</h2>
      <p>
        Partitioning SNP-heritability across <strong>63 independent GWAS</strong>, the most constrained common
        variants are the most enriched for disease heritability. The comparison table is the paper's strongest
        claim:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Annotation</th>
              <th>SNP-heritability enrichment</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Constrained in primates</strong></td>
              <td><strong>11.1-fold</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Constrained in mammals</strong></td>
              <td><strong>7.8-fold</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Nonsynonymous coding variants</td>
              <td>7.2-fold</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Fine-mapped eQTL SNPs</td>
              <td>4.8-fold</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Why the ordering is surprising
        </div>
        <p>
          Nonsynonymous coding variants are the canonical "obviously functional" class, and fine-mapped eQTLs
          are the canonical regulatory class. Mammalian constraint <strong>exceeds both</strong>, and primate
          constraint exceeds it further still — 11.1-fold against 7.8. The primate result is the more
          interesting one: it says that the closer the comparison species, the more the constraint signal
          reflects specifically human-relevant function, at the cost of less total branch length to measure it
          with. That trade-off is the same one every comparative-genomics resource has to price.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="scales" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Informative at Every Scale, from 100 kb to One Base</h2>
      <p>
        The paper stresses that the alignment is informative across a range of scales rather than at one
        preferred resolution:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>100-kb bins</strong> — exceptionally constrained regions include <em>all</em> the HOX clusters, which is the kind of result that validates the method by recovering what is already known.</li>
        <li><strong>Ultraconserved elements and human accelerated regions</strong> — the two opposite tails, one where change was forbidden and one where it was unusually rapid on the human lineage.</li>
        <li><strong>Noncoding regulatory regions</strong> — enhancers and promoters, the class GWAS keeps landing in.</li>
        <li><strong>Specific base positions within binding motifs</strong> — the finest scale, where constraint distinguishes the positions a transcription factor actually reads from the ones it does not.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation: phyloP and Heritability Partitioning</h2>

      <h3>phyloP as a likelihood ratio per base</h3>
      <p>
        phyloP tests, at each base, whether the observed pattern of substitutions across the phylogeny departs
        from a neutral model. It is a likelihood-ratio statistic comparing a neutral rate to a fitted rate
        <Katex tex="\rho" /> at that column of the alignment:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\text{phyloP} = -\operatorname{sign}(\rho - 1)\,\log_{10} p, \qquad \Lambda = -2\left[\log L(\text{neutral}) - \log L(\hat{\rho})\right]" />
      </div>
      <p>
        The sign convention carries real information: <Katex tex="\rho < 1" /> means fewer substitutions than
        neutral (constraint, positive score) and <Katex tex="\rho > 1" /> means more (acceleration, negative
        score). Human accelerated regions are the negative tail of the same statistic that finds constraint.
      </p>

      <h3>Stratified heritability</h3>
      <p>
        Enrichment is defined as the share of SNP-heritability captured by an annotation divided by the share
        of SNPs it contains:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\text{Enrichment}(C) = \frac{h^2_g(C) \,/\, h^2_g}{\;|C| \,/\, M\;}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        A value of 1 means the annotation carries heritability in proportion to its size. The 7.8-fold figure
        says constrained bases carry nearly eight times the heritability their genomic footprint would
        predict — and because constraint covers only 3.3% of the genome, the footprint is small.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & What Constraint Cannot Tell You</h2>

      <h3>What constraint is blind to</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Recently evolved function.</strong> A regulatory element that arose on the human or primate lineage has had too little time to accumulate a constraint signal across mammals. The 11.1-fold primate result is partly a response to this, but the same logic applies again within primates.</li>
        <li><strong>Direction and mechanism.</strong> Constraint says a base matters. It does not say which allele is deleterious, in which tissue, through which gene, or by how much — everything a therapeutic hypothesis needs.</li>
        <li><strong>Function without conservation.</strong> Some genuinely functional sequence turns over rapidly while retaining function; constraint scores it as neutral.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Statistical cautions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Enrichment is not causality.</strong> Stratified heritability enrichment is a property of an annotation across many loci; it licenses using constraint as a prior, not calling any individual constrained variant causal.</li>
        <li><strong>The alignment is the model.</strong> phyloP inherits every assumption in the phylogeny, the neutral rate estimate, and the alignment itself — and alignment quality is worst exactly in the repetitive and rapidly evolving regions where the answer would be most interesting.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Source note
        </div>
        <p>
          Written against the full text of the author manuscript deposited as PMC10259825. This page cites
          <strong>240 mammals</strong>, which is the paper's own figure; an earlier version said 241. The
          companion resource paper — Christmas et al., <em>Evolutionary constraint and innovation across
          hundreds of placental mammals</em> — is summarised at
          <a href="/papers/zoonomia-constraint/" style="color: var(--color-accent);">/papers/zoonomia-constraint/</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
